ref_solution:
The prime factorization of $432 = 2^4 \cdot 3^3$. It follows that the sum of the divisors is equal to $(1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 3 + 3^2 + 3^3)$, as each factor of $432$ is represented when the product is expanded. We have to subtract $432$ so that we only count the proper divisors, so the answer is \begin{align*}
(1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 3 + 9 + 27) - 432 &= (31)(40) - 432\\
&= 1240 - 432\\
&= \boxed{808}.\\
\end{align*}

To solve for $x$, we wish to eliminate $y$. Multiply the first equation by $2$ and add it to the second: \begin{align*}
(4x-2y) + (x+2y) &= 10+5\\
5x &= 15\\
x &= \boxed{3}
\end{align*}

Since $\left(\frac{1}{4}, -6\right)$ lies on the line, we plug $x = \frac{1}{4}$ and $y = -6$ into the equation to get  \begin{align*}
-\frac{1}{2} - \frac{k}{2} &= 5(-6)\\
\Rightarrow\qquad -1-k = -60\\
\Rightarrow\qquad k=\boxed{59}.
\end{align*}

